# gitleaks false positives, allowlisted by fingerprint.
#
# Format: <commit>:<file>:<rule>:<line>. A fingerprint pins one finding in one
# historical commit, so allowlisting here cannot hide a NEW secret in the same
# file — which is why this is the right mechanism rather than widening the rule
# or adding a path exclusion.
#
# Every entry must record what the match actually was and why it is not a
# secret. An unexplained fingerprint is indistinguishable from a suppressed
# real leak.
#
# Do NOT quote a flagged string verbatim in this file. The history scan reads
# every commit, so a quoted sample in an explanatory comment becomes a finding
# of its own. This has already happened twice elsewhere in the estate.

# The headless telemetry test seeds a store with an installation id so it can
# assert what a run with consent already given transmits. The value was a
# randomly generated v4 UUID, and `generic-api-key` decides on the entropy of
# the value rather than on what the key means — so a random identifier reads
# the same to it as a credential would. It is not one: installation ids are
# minted on the device, never issued, and this one was typed into a test.
#
# The live fixtures now use a structurally obvious placeholder id, so the
# working-tree scan needs no entry at all. Only history does, and only for the
# commit that introduced the random one. That is the order to prefer wherever
# it is available: remove what trips the rule, and allowlist only what history
# has frozen.
